## Scope and confidence

This review uses the support export dated **4 October 2026**. It is a snapshot, not the live desk.
The desk may have received reports or changed tickets since then. Reconcile against current
ticket IDs and merge history before acting on the queue.

The [original incident page](/kb/incidents/4471) calls this one parser bug. Its own rendered
table separates two causes. Reading the report bodies reveals timing anomalies that the
table's classifier cannot detect. The split below is proposed; no engineering fix or support
decision is claimed to have been approved.

## Reviewed report allocation

<ReviewedReports />

## What needs fixing or investigating

### Parser: respect the input's date order

The shared parser reads dates month first. A day-first tenant's app or email input can
therefore swap day and month. For example, R-41200 reports 7 April becoming 4 July, and
R-41203 reports 2 December becoming 12 February.

Confirm the input contract for each channel and parse according to that contract; reject
invalid or ambiguous input rather than silently swapping it. Verify both ambiguous dates
and days greater than twelve. Tenant locale comes from the directory, not the reporter's browser.

### Form hint: match the tenant's date order

For day-first tenants using booking-v3, the form prints `MM/DD/YYYY`, while the form reads
dates in the tenant's order. A person following the hint gets a swapped date. R-41230 is
one example: copper.studio, booking-v3, form channel, 6 January versus 1 June. Intake tagged
that report `parser`, demonstrating why intake tags do not determine the split.

Align the hint with the form's parsing contract and test the actual displayed field and
submitted value together. Fixing the shared parser alone does not correct misleading instructions.

### Timing: investigate without assuming a root cause

The evidence table preserves all five reports. One-day shifts and different days in Lisbon
and Toronto could involve time zones or date-only conversions. An invite arriving before its
booking could instead involve scheduling or delivery order. These are hypotheses, not findings.

Obtain event and booking IDs, creation and send timestamps with offsets, tenant and recipient
time zones, original invite payloads, calendar display settings, and delivery logs. Reproduce
each symptom, including June-only behavior. Split this provisional investigation further if
the evidence establishes different mechanisms. Do not claim a timezone fix has been proven.

## What the existing components actually compute

<CauseTable ticket={4471} />

The classifier uses tenant locale, channel, and form version. It never reads the report body.
Consequently it assigns the timing anomalies to `parser`. Its totals differ from the reviewed
allocation above for that explicit reason. Browser locale and `meta.intakeTag` are intake guesses.
Deduplicate export rows by report ID, not by identical body or subject.

## When reports arrived versus when they were merged

<QueueTimeline ticket={4471} />

The chart shows received and merged dates separately. A bulk merge on 2 July is not a sudden
onset: some reports had arrived months earlier. Continued merges through the export's last day
also mean that a queued fix is not evidence of resolution. Use receivedAt for symptom history
and mergedAt for desk activity.

## Proposed triage rule

The [accepted decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject) and
[triage prose](/kb/triage/dedupe) date from 2024. They match subjects without reading bodies.
Preserve that accepted record as history; this page proposes a replacement for desk approval:

1. Read the body and establish the observed versus expected behavior, tenant, locale, channel,
   and form version. Ask for missing context.
2. Use a matching subject only to find candidates. Merge after verifying the same failure
   mechanism; different subjects can still describe the same mechanism.
3. Keep distinct mechanisms separate, even with identical subjects. With unknown tenant or
   contradictory evidence, keep the report unclassified and investigate rather than force a merge.
4. Count unique report IDs once. Preserve provenance and link related investigations.
5. Re-read the rendered views and reconcile the live queue before publishing current totals.

<DedupeExplorer />

The explorer demonstrates why subject matching fails: the same tenant and subject can produce
different causes through different channels, while different subjects can share a cause.
It too is limited to the classifier's inputs and cannot settle the timing investigation.

<Callout kind="warn">
  Treat narrative, tags, and computed views as evidence with limits. Check the underlying
  reports before treating a classification as a diagnosis. These snapshot counts do not
  establish the current state of the desk.
</Callout>

